{
 "_about": {
  "what": "Final brand-kit tokens for the Desk app, Platform and Guild apps and the sign-in pages. Every token in web/src/theme/tokens.ts on main at 1332067, all three themes.",
  "how_to_read": "Each entry: final, today, changed. kit_exact marks a value taken unchanged from the kit palette in docs/assets/brand/README.md. The status and badge groups are unchanged on purpose.",
  "etch": "main still has etch.top and etch.bottom and the three-pass draw of ADR 0062. ADR 0064 renames them to etch.wall and etch.highlight with a four-step cut. Use wall and highlight with that code. etch.top and etch.bottom are here only for a build that lands the colors before ADR 0064, and they go away with the rename. adr0064 shows the values that ADR set against the old panels.",
  "dusk": "Zen (the theme id stays `dusk`; ADR 0093, 2026-11-10, cooled the same evening on the owner's \"I hate brown\"): a cool slate office in MA's blue-grey with wood only on the desks, cool near-whites (#F1F3F7, #DFE3EA, #D3D8E0; they replace ADR 0050's neutral whites for this theme), MA's blue softened as the accent, a soft green for online and a cool gold for warn. Entries marked adr0093 changed with it; their `today` is the Dusk value they replaced. The desk textures in this theme are low-chroma wood (web/src/assets/desk-styles/desk-styles.json).",
  "status": "Draft. Nothing is committed.",
  "counts": {
   "light": {
    "tokens": 54,
    "changed": 40
   },
   "dusk": {
    "tokens": 54,
    "changed": 62
   },
   "dark": {
    "tokens": 54,
    "changed": 45
   }
  },
  "tileStacked": "New for ADR 0064: only a tile that overlaps one below it, or is being dragged. Not yet judged in the app.",
  "additions": "2026-11-07, kit additions from the owner's decisions in DECISIONS.md (Notebook look 6 and 7 of 9). Not in web/src/theme/tokens.ts yet; the Notebook keeps its own copies in web/notebook/src/theme/tokens.ts, which these match. Marked added:true, with today:null. text.danger is for destructive words (Delete); status.error stays for dots and badges. notebook.* are the binder tab colors and the highlight; inside a highlight, links and ticked items use text.primary and keep their underline. Names are two-part (notebook.tabBlue, not notebook.tab.blue) so a reader that splits on the first dot, as web/notebook/demo/brandThemes.ts does, makes --ma-notebook-tab-blue and not a clash. The counts above don't include them.",
  "backdrop": "2026-11-10, ADR 0094 (office backgrounds): surface.backdrop is the scrim over a background scene, composed as surface.backdropBlend says. Zen and Dark: a navy that multiplies the picture (mix-blend-mode: multiply), never a lightening wash: the owner (Nov 10, 9:30 pm MT) kept the deep dark blues and had the picture itself toned down in the art pipeline (grade v8); Zen alpha 0, a no-op darken, since the rings' rule holds on the bare toned picture and the owner called anything darker depressing (the approved look is the graded photo under the navy band only); Dark 0.30 the lowest at which the rings' rule holds under a multiply on the v8 set. Light: a light wash, source-over, 0.40, the lowest that passes on the v8 set (moonlit-sea, a night scene, sets it) (a light colour multiplies to nothing, and a dark multiply would put a near-black ground under a light UI). No text sits on bare ground. ring.halo is a 1 px dark line outside the active desk's ring and the focus ring while a scene shows: the ring, or the halo, reaches 3:1 on the ground, and the ring always reaches 3:1 on its halo. Measured in web/src/theme/backgrounds.test.ts, composed per theme; measured on the v8 set (seven scenes, averages #0a1837 to #4a6278); the manifest's grade.scrim {mode: none} is the pipeline's note that the photo is pre-toned, and the themes' tokens decide the scrim in the app.",
  "backdropBand": "2026-11-10, ADR 0094: surface.backdropBand is the edge bands under a background's scrim, the same navy in every theme: the top and bottom edges fading to transparent at 14% of the height, no side bands, under the flat layer on the same pseudo-element, multiplied with the picture like the rest of it. The art pipeline measures text contrast in those bands with exactly this definition, and a manifest's grade.edge_band {alpha, top_bottom, sides, color} overrides it at runtime (sides 0 draws none).",
  "badges": "Unread counts use the accent in every theme; red means errors only (the Lead's call, Nov 10, with ADR 0125's notifications). Light and Dark finals are the final accent; today the office uses its today accent."
 },
